A||N||12||terryk||4T60E Shift cable||||1||
Z||000000||terryk||10-16-2000||01:07 AM||||Best laid plans........<P>So for those of you with 4T60E's, how was the shift cable handled? It operates backward from the stock Fiero (or normal for a normal car). If the lever on the trans is swapped right to left on the shaft, it hits the crossover/rear header.<P>I figured out a solution, but I'm curious what others have done. Might be something cleaner.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||10-16-2000||04:32 AM||||What was your solution?<p>[This message has been edited by Monkeyman (edited 10-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||10-16-2000||02:54 PM||||Actually two,<P>1. A 9 inch extention on the lever to move it above the crossover. Looks odd....The cable goes just below the TB.<P>2. A direction reverser (new word!) A pivoting arm with a slot to move the selector. The mockup works, but the throw is off. I might go with this if I can. Otherwise, it new custom cables.||||reg||1
Z||000003||SeanyD||10-16-2000||05:07 PM||||may seem like a dumb idea....but could just changine the shift pattern with the stock cables work out for ya? would add a new uniqueness to the car!||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||10-16-2000||05:26 PM||||Trust me, a 123ODNRP is a real possibility.<P>This is not insurmountable, I just want to make sure I didn't ignore a nice clean solution. The top PRNDL indicator is already changed to PRNOD321, but that could be changed "again".<P>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||10-16-2000||06:20 PM||||Try the arm from a Fiero TH125 on it? ||||reg||1
Z||000006||rockcrawl||10-16-2000||07:01 PM|||| I asked the forum about this before, but the question was mixed with another topic and I never got any answers. I ran into the same problem with my Ferrari replica, but I have decided to go to a manual trans, so I gave up on it. I was going to buy a Morse cable that is about 2 ft longer than the Fiero cable and just run it past the trans and loop back. It would require a new bracket be fabricated to mount the cable to the trans, but it eliminates any play that would be added with a "direction reverser" (which I also considered). I figured that would be the cleanest and most reliable solution, and probalbly the easiest. The cable could also be looped at the front instead, and connect to the front of the shifter for an even cleaner install.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"||||reg||10
Z||000007||Dan86gt3.8||10-16-2000||09:30 PM||||ummm terry, what we had to do was wled an arm that came out and held the lump on the cable at about a 45 degree angle to the tranny.  but its always never gone past 3rd (meaning I cannot shift it all the way down to 2nd and 1st gear)  this has never really bugged me seing as I do not feel the need to yank it down that far.  We could most likely cure this with a cable that was about 2" longer... why have I not done this?  probably the same reason why I never figured up a way to rig a dogbone.... TO LAZY!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Dan86gt3.8||10-16-2000||09:34 PM||||Ohhhh by the way teryyy, e-mail me your problems cause everything you have ran into so far I have run into when we did my swap.  No offense to the board of course  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  I also don't check the board regularly so I usually don't catch theese topics till they are dead...||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||10-16-2000||09:44 PM||||Will do Dan.<P>Looks like I'm going to go with a second lever that has a link to the first. I want it to run through all of the pattern since the console is now mod'd to look factory.<P>This way the cable essentially goes in the same direction. The adapter will bolt to the two bolts that hold the switch assembly.  <P>You're running a Series II right?<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 10-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Dan86gt3.8||10-17-2000||02:22 AM||||ya I have a series 2.  Are you saying you put in a different shifter? "modified console"???? We used the stock fiero shifter...||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||10-17-2000||11:35 AM||||I haven't seen that tranny in awhile....<P>In all seriousness.... did you try the stock Fiero TH125 arm & hardware? It might need a little work but it's likely closer than adding levers. Knowing how GM and others like to keep inventory down it might even drop right on the thing.<P>Another option would be cut the eye off the arm and weld the thing back together where it doesn't hit things. You have to watch the lenght of the arm and be a decent welder but there's no reason you couldn't do it. Done carfully no one would ever know it wasnt made that way.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||terryk||10-17-2000||11:42 AM||||Hi Theogre,<P>I've tried levers and brackets from about 10 different applications including the Fiero. The cable is too short to just rotate around the back. <P>The 125C selector shaft is positioned farther away from the engine so there is clearance to swing on the engine side. On the Series I, the crossover is pretty close to the shaft. I noticed that ACE uses the 10ft extention to raise the cable up and clear the crossover on a Series II. <P>If I didn't want to select 1,2 and maybe 3, none of this would be a problem. <P>I still have options. I'm just not jumping to the first solution that works.<P>Also, the lever ratio's need to remain equal between the console and trans or nothing lines up.<P>Keep 'em coming though!<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 10-17-2000).]||||reg||1
